<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- The id NEVER changes. It is what every installed box, the repository
     catalog and the hosted mirror key on, and "pythonfix" is now narrower than
     what this add-on does: 1.1.0 absorbed the Siri remote keymap that
     skin.estuary.pov wrote through 1.2.8. The display name below carries the
     truth; the id carries the continuity. Renaming the id would orphan every
     box that already has this installed. -->
<addon id="service.tvos.pythonfix" version="1.1.0" name="Apple TV Fixes" provider-name="Tony.7.Bones">
	<requires>
		<import addon="xbmc.python" version="3.0.0"/>
		<!-- NOT decoration, and NOT a library this add-on imports. It is the
		     LOCATION GUARANTEE for the _scproxy shim.

		     The shim is written into the library directories of this add-on and
		     of its own four dependencies, because those are the only writable
		     directories that are on the sys.path of every add-on the defect
		     actually kills. Declaring this one means Kodi has finished
		     installing the whole subtree (certifi, chardet, idna, urllib3, then
		     requests) before this add-on's service runs, so every one of those
		     directories is guaranteed to exist.

		     ONE import, not five, and that is deliberate. Kodi has no minversion
		     attribute here, so every <import> line is a hard version FLOOR that
		     somebody has to maintain, and the other four are already pulled in
		     transitively by this one. scripts/service.py names all five
		     directories but SKIPS any that is absent, so if requests ever drops
		     a dependency this add-on adapts instead of failing a version check.

		     Ordering is no longer part of the reason. This add-on is installed
		     by the user, on its own, before anything that needs it. It was a
		     sibling dependency of skin.estuary.pov in 1.2.7 only, and that is
		     over. -->
		<import addon="script.module.requests" version="2.31.0"/>
	</requires>
	<!-- ONE extension point, and no <provides>. Both are the invisibility, and
	     both were measured against the Kodi 22 source and on a clean bench
	     because the owner asked for this to stay out of the user interface.

	     No <provides>: CAddonInfo::HasType is mainType OR ProvidesSubContent
	     (AddonInfo.cpp:193-197) and GenerateMainCategoryListing builds its
	     categories from HasType, so adding <provides>video would put this in
	     "My add-ons > Video add-ons" as well. Absent is already optimal.

	     WHERE IT STILL SHOWS, since Kodi offers no way to hide an add-on: as a
	     row inside Settings > Add-ons > My add-ons > Services, and in that
	     screen's All list. It adds NO new section. Kodi bundles its own
	     service.xbmc.versioncheck, so the Services category is on every install
	     already, and this is a second row in a list nobody browses. Nothing
	     appears anywhere on a skin's Add-ons home tab: those five rows are
	     addons://sources/<type>/, which queries only PLUGIN, SCRIPT and GAMEDLL
	     (AddonsDirectory.cpp:915-949), so a service cannot reach them. -->
	<extension point="xbmc.service" library="scripts/service.py" start="startup"/>
	<extension point="xbmc.addon.metadata">
		<!-- all, and do NOT narrow it to tvos. "tvos" IS a valid platform tag
		     (AddonInfoBuilder.cpp:875-877), so nothing rejects the edit and the
		     damage is invisible until a box tries to install this. Narrowing it
		     is the obvious-looking way to keep a tvOS-only add-on off other
		     boxes, and it is wrong for two reasons that outlive whoever declares
		     it.

		     ONE: this add-on is USER-INSTALLED, from a repository listing.
		     PlatformSupportsAddon returning false makes CAddonInfoBuilder::Generate
		     return nullptr (AddonInfoBuilder.cpp:224-243) for BOTH the local scan
		     and the repository addons.xml parse. That is not "hidden on the wrong
		     platform"; it is absent from the repository listing entirely, and it
		     is also the parse of the WHOLE addons.xml, which is shared by every
		     box. Measured on a bench with <platform>tvos</platform>: "No platform
		     for add-on service.tvos.pythonfix", followed by "CAddonDatabase:
		     service.tvos.pythonfix has been uninstalled" - Kodi tears the add-on
		     out of its database rather than leaving it dormant. An add-on Kodi
		     refuses to build an info object for cannot be listed, installed or
		     updated by anybody, on any box, and the one platform that needs it
		     would get it only by luck of the repo cache.

		     TWO, and this is the reason that is CURRENT rather than historical:
		     narrowing it would make this add-on UNVERIFIABLE. The wipeable macOS
		     Kodi bench is where this project does its cheapest and most-used
		     testing, and the standalone install path is now the ONLY way anybody
		     gets this add-on, so it is the one path that must be provable. With
		     <platform>tvos</platform> the add-on cannot even be browsed to on
		     macOS and that test cannot be run at all.

		     An earlier revision of this comment argued from skin.estuary.pov
		     1.2.7's hard <import>, which made a narrowed platform fail the
		     SKIN's install on every non-tvOS box. That import is gone as of skin
		     1.2.8 and that argument is void. It is recorded here only so nobody
		     reads its absence as the ban having expired: the two reasons above
		     never rested on any skin.

		     The gate that actually matters is the runtime one in
		     scripts/service.py, System.Platform.TVOS, and it is enforced by
		     tests. Off tvOS this add-on starts, checks that one condition and
		     returns, which is what "does nothing on other platforms" costs. -->
		<platform>all</platform>
		<license>GPL-2.0-or-later</license>
		<source>https://github.com/moquette/kodi-estuary-pov</source>
		<!-- ONE asset, and the decision recorded at 1.0.1 ("no <assets> yet,
		     deliberately") is REVERSED here rather than silently dropped. The
		     old reason was that the only icon in this repo belonged to the skin
		     and shipping a skin's artwork on a service would be wrong; the
		     answer to that was to draw this add-on one of its own, not to go
		     without. Kodi 22 has NO implicit icon.png fallback: AddonInfoBuilder
		     sets m_icon only from this block (AddonInfoBuilder.cpp:432-471), so
		     without it the row is the generic DefaultAddon.png placeholder and
		     an owner cannot tell at a glance that the box has this on it.

		     512x512 RGBA PNG, which is the convention this repo uses for scripts
		     and services (script.ezmaintenanceplusplus, repository.tony7bones).
		     256x256 RGB is the SKIN convention and this is not a skin.

		     No <fanart>, no <screenshot>, no <banner>, no <clearlogo>: fanart
		     renders as the background of the add-on info dialog, a screen nobody
		     reaches for a service with no UI, and one would be 30x the size of
		     this entire add-on.

		     No settings screen either, and that is a decision rather than an
		     omission. There is nothing to configure: one behaviour, two fixed
		     targets, one platform gate. The only version with real value is a
		     read-only "last written" status line, and that would need a
		     setSetting call on every boot, which means a write into this
		     add-on's own addon_data on tvOS. That storage class has produced
		     four separate bugs on these boxes (Apple TV playbook sections 8 and
		     11). The evidence already exists and costs nothing: one INFO line in
		     kodi.log on every tvOS start. -->
		<assets>
			<icon>resources/icon.png</icon>
		</assets>
		<news>[B]1.1.0[/B][CR]- The Siri remote fixes moved here from the Estuary POV skin, so they now work on Apple TV whatever skin you use, and Estuary POV no longer carries any Apple TV code at all. Back leaves fullscreen video with playback continuing, double play/pause returns to it, select opens the OSD on live TV, and back at Home no longer opens the Favourites window.[CR]- The Python repair is now written to five places instead of one, so updating any single add-on can no longer take it away and leave your other add-ons broken until the next restart.[CR]- Renamed from "tvOS Python Fix" to "Apple TV Fixes", because it is no longer only about Python. Nothing needs reinstalling; it is the same add-on.[CR]- Dropped a weather refresh that ran on every start and never did anything. Kodi drops that request unless the Weather window is the one on screen, which at startup it never is. The service now finishes the moment both files are written instead of waiting ten seconds.[CR]- It has an icon now, so you can see it in your add-on list.[CR]- Still does nothing at all on Fire TV, Android, Windows, Linux or macOS.[CR][CR][B]1.0.1[/B][CR]- No change in what it does. Documentation only: the measured margin this add-on wins by was recorded as seconds and is actually milliseconds, and getting that wrong in a comment is how the ordering it depends on gets removed later.[CR][CR][B]1.0.0[/B][CR]- First release. On Apple TV, puts back the piece of Python that Kodi 22's Apple TV build leaves out, so every add-on that fetches anything over the internet works again. Without it POV, Multi Weather and EZ Maintenance++ all fail the moment they start.[CR]- Does nothing at all on Fire TV, Android, Windows, Linux or macOS.</news>
		<summary lang="en_GB">Everything Kodi&apos;s Apple TV build needs put right, in one add-on.</summary>
		<description lang="en_GB">Kodi&apos;s Apple TV build has two problems that no skin and no other add-on can fix, and this puts both right on every start.[CR][CR]It ships a Python with the _scproxy module removed while Python carries on reporting that it is running on macOS, so anything that imports urllib.request or requests fails the moment it starts, which is every add-on that fetches anything over the internet. This writes a stand-in for the missing module, and writes it again if an add-on update removes it.[CR][CR]It also maps the Siri remote in ways that fight you: back stops playback outright in fullscreen video, with no button that returns to fullscreen, and back at the home screen opens the Favourites window instead of doing nothing. This writes a keymap that fixes all of it.[CR][CR]Install it once on an Apple TV, before anything else, and forget it. It does nothing at all on any other platform.</description>
		<disclaimer lang="en_GB">A workaround for defects in Kodi&apos;s own Apple TV build. Delete this add-on once a Kodi build ships that no longer needs it.</disclaimer>
	</extension>
</addon>
